[HOOK]
How can this game handle so much lighting and so many shadows, and still run efficiently?

[THE PROBLEM]
Light is crazy expensive to compute. When Godot bakes lighting, it shoots rays out from every single pixel of the lightmap,
 and each ray bounces around the scene to figure out what light it finds. 
 Now imagine doing that 60 times a second. Yeah, no.

[THE TRICK]
So here’s the trick: games don’t calculate it live. They cheat. It’s called baked lighting.

[HOW IT WORKS]
Before the game ever ships, Godot simulates all the light in your scene. 
Where it hits, where it bounces, where it’s blocked. Then it paints the result 
straight onto a texture. Shadows, soft bounce light, all of it, just stored as an image.

[TABLE EXAMPLE]
Here’s what that looks like for this table. This is its lightmap texture. That’s 
he whole light simulation, flattened into one image. And that’s all Godot has to 
ship with the game. The table now has this lighting texture, and no lights are being rendered at all.

[THE PAYOFF]
So the game isn’t doing any lighting math. It’s just displaying a texture. That’s basically free.

[THE CATCH]
But because it’s baked in, say I have this floor with an object on it. The shadow is 
painted onto the floor’s texture. If I move the object, the shadow stays right where 
it was, because it’s literally just paint on the floor. That’s why you only use baked 
lighting for static objects, things that never move. Anything that moves won’t cast a 
shadow on baked surfaces, so you use real-time lighting for those and mix the two.

[OUTRO]
So next time a game looks insanely good and runs smooth, the lighting might just be a 
really pretty painting. Follow for more game dev tricks!